# 📦 Array in JavaScript

> **Array = an ordered collection of values stored under one variable.**

---

# 1. 🧠 What is an Array?

Instead of creating separate variables:

```js
let student1 = "Rahul";
let student2 = "Aman";
let student3 = "Priya";
let student4 = "Neha";
```

We can use an array:

```js
let students = ["Rahul", "Aman", "Priya", "Neha"];
```

### Visual representation

```text
                    ARRAY
                      │
                      ↓
        ┌───────┬───────┬───────┬───────┐
        │ Rahul │ Aman  │ Priya │ Neha  │
        └───────┴───────┴───────┴───────┘
            0       1       2       3
            ↑                       ↑
          index                   index
```

> ⚠️ JavaScript arrays are **zero-indexed**.

So:

```text
First element  → index 0
Second element → index 1
Third element  → index 2
Fourth element → index 3
```

---

# 2. 🏗️ Creating an Array

### Array literal — most common

```js
let fruits = ["Apple", "Banana", "Mango"];
```

### Using `new Array()`

```js
let fruits = new Array("Apple", "Banana", "Mango");
```

Usually prefer:

```js
let fruits = ["Apple", "Banana", "Mango"];
```

---

# 3. 🔍 Accessing Array Elements

```js
let fruits = ["Apple", "Banana", "Mango"];

console.log(fruits[0]); // Apple
console.log(fruits[1]); // Banana
console.log(fruits[2]); // Mango
```

Diagram:

```text
              fruits
                 │
                 ↓
        ┌────────┬────────┬────────┐
        │ Apple  │ Banana │ Mango  │
        └────────┴────────┴────────┘
             0        1        2
             ↑        ↑        ↑
           fruits[0] fruits[1] fruits[2]
```

---

# 4. ✏️ Changing an Element

Arrays are mutable.

```js
let fruits = ["Apple", "Banana", "Mango"];

fruits[1] = "Orange";

console.log(fruits);
```

Output:

```text
["Apple", "Orange", "Mango"]
```

Before:

```text
┌─────────┬──────────┬─────────┐
│  Apple  │  Banana  │  Mango  │
└─────────┴──────────┴─────────┘
     0          1         2
```

After:

```text
┌─────────┬──────────┬─────────┐
│  Apple  │  Orange  │  Mango  │
└─────────┴──────────┴─────────┘
     0          1         2
```

---

# 5. 📏 Array Length

Use:

```js
let fruits = ["Apple", "Banana", "Mango"];

console.log(fruits.length); // 3
```

Diagram:

```text
        ┌────────┬────────┬────────┐
        │ Apple  │ Banana │ Mango  │
        └────────┴────────┴────────┘
             0        1        2

              ↑
           3 elements

fruits.length === 3
```

### Important

```text
Last index = length - 1
```

Example:

```js
let arr = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];

arr.length; // 5

arr[4];     // 50
```

